### 4. Competing Approaches

#### 4.1 C: `malloc`/`free` and Custom Allocators

C’s standard library provides `malloc`, `free`, `realloc` as global functions. While you can write custom allocators and expose them via a struct of function pointers, the language provides no standard interface, no type‑safe generics, and no help with alignment. Every C library invents its own allocator API (`foo_alloc`, `foo_free`), leading to fragmentation.

Zig’s `Allocator` is exactly that standard interface, but it is baked into the standard library, type‑safe, and alignment‑aware.

#### 4.2 Rust: Global Allocator and Allocator API

Rust has a `GlobalAlloc` trait that can be set globally. The `Allocator` trait is unstable and not yet integrated into the standard collections. In practice, most Rust code uses the global allocator implicitly via `Box`, `Vec`, etc. Custom allocators require nightly features and careful trait object boxing, and even then, many standard library types do not support them. Zig’s solution is simpler: a single concrete `Allocator` type that is stable, always available, and trivially composable.

#### 4.3 C++: Allocator Template Parameters

C++ containers like `std::vector<T, A>` accept an allocator type as a template parameter. While this allows per‑container custom allocation, it comes at a heavy cost: different allocator types produce different container types, breaking ABI and complicating interfaces. Zig’s runtime vtable approach avoids template proliferation; one function signature accepts any allocator without monomorphisation.

#### 4.4 Go: Garbage Collector Only

Go’s memory model is built around a garbage collector. There is no standard way to manually allocate or use arenas without unsafe packages and manual memory copying. Zig’s model gives you the choice: you can build your own GC on top of the allocator interface if you wish, but you are never forced to pay for one.

